\begin{center}
{\bfseries\large Abstract}\vspace{0.3cm}
\end{center}
\noindent The advent of Large Language Models (LLMs) has revolutionized literature exploration and documentary synthesis. However, their integration into social and economic research encounters two critical impediments: ethical vulnerabilities associated with transmitting sensitive qualitative and microeconomic data to external cloud infrastructures, and academic invalidity stemming from hallucinations and unverified citations. This paper introduces \textbf{AcadAId}, a 100\% offline, on-device Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) system engineered to execute on commodity consumer hardware. The architecture leverages a quantized \textit{Qwen 2.5 3B Instruct} model (Q4\_K\_M) powered by \textit{llama.cpp}, integrated with an embedded vector store (\textit{ChromaDB}) and dense semantic embeddings (\textit{all-MiniLM-L6-v2}). To enforce scientific rigor, the pipeline implements Maximal Marginal Relevance (MMR) retrieval coupled with a deterministic post-processing engine that suppresses text degeneration and ensures paragraph-level attribution with exact file and page citations. The findings confirm that localized inference provides high hermeneutic fidelity and bibliographic accountability, safeguarding data confidentiality and research ethics in empirical social and economic disciplines.
\vspace{0.25cm}

\noindent \textbf{Keywords:} Local Artificial Intelligence, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), Research Methodology, Social Sciences, Economics, Data Privacy, Hallucination Mitigation, Academic Traceability.

